# Shared torrc for Dana's Tor egress pool (mounted read-only into every
# peterdavehello/tor-socks-proxy instance at /etc/tor/torrc).
#
# SearXNG routes EVERY engine request through this pool (see
# searxng/settings.yml outgoing.proxies) and round-robins across the instances
# per-request. Search-engine blocking is PER-IP, so we beat it two ways:
#   1. run MANY instances (more simultaneous exit IPs)              -> docker-compose.yml
#   2. rotate each instance's exit IP FAST (MaxCircuitDirtiness)    -> here
#
# We bind-mount this file because the image's CMD is `tor -f /etc/tor/torrc`,
# giving us deterministic control of every directive (the old dperson/torproxy
# silently dropped TOR_MaxCircuitDirtiness due to an upstream entrypoint bug).

# --- upstream essentials (kept identical to the image's baked-in torrc) ---
HardwareAccel 1
Log notice stdout
DataDirectory /var/lib/tor
SocksPort 0.0.0.0:9150
DNSPort 0.0.0.0:8853

# --- fast IP rotation: cycle each exit ~every 60s to dodge per-IP blocks ---
# MaxCircuitDirtiness: max age a circuit keeps serving NEW streams before tor
# switches to a fresh circuit (=> new exit IP). 60s is the low end of the
# verified 60-120s window; Tor warns below ~60s, so this is the safe minimum.
MaxCircuitDirtiness 60
# Pre-build a replacement circuit on this cadence so rotation is seamless.
NewCircuitPeriod 30
# Require each circuit's relays to live in distinct /16 subnets => the exit IPs
# we present to engines are genuinely different, not near-duplicates.
EnforceDistinctSubnets 1
